ah, the classic "my console's memory is worse than my own" bug. it's almost always because your `console.log` is running *after* the line that sets the new value.

think of the console as a slow-moving, high-stakes newsreader. when you type `console.log`, it's just saying "hey, can I have a snippet of data from right now?" but it doesn't get the answer until *after* it's already said it. so if you're setting a new value on line 5, then logging it out on line 10, you're getting the old value.

the fix is to wrap your log statement in another function, or use `setTimeout` to make sure it runs immediately after the update:

```javascript
const myVar = 'hello';
console.log(myVar); // prints "hello"

// this is the fix:
setTimeout(() => {
    console.log(myVar); // prints "world", the updated value
}, 0);
```

or

```javascript
const myVar = 'hello';
console.log('before', myVar);

setImmediate(() => {
    console.log('after', myVar); // prints "world", the updated value
});

myVar = 'world';
``

ah, the classic 'I'm so close, I can taste it' moment that feels like a betrayal from your browser. it's almost certainly because you've fallen for React's most insidious lie: that it runs in the same thread as the rest of the page.

when you change a component, React needs to re-render everything. that's its job. but it doesn't have to do it one by one. it could just... rebuild the whole damn page. this is called "full document hydration" or "full document reconciliation." it sounds impressive, but it means your entire DOM tree gets deleted and rebuilt from scratch, which is fucking slow and makes for an absolutely miserable user experience.

the fix is to force React to render *just* the changed part of the page. you do this with `shouldComponentUpdate`. think of it as React's little warning bell: "hey, before you re-render this massive thing, are you sure you only need to update this tiny bit?" if the answer is yes, it'll stop rebuilding the whole lot.

so, in your component file, you'd put something like:

```jsx
shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps, nextState) {
  return nextProps.changed !== undefined;
}
```

